Construct a complete, production-ready cloud-based development platform that provides an integrated environment for creating, editing, collaborating on, and deploying applications. The platform must include AI-powered tools for code generation and assistance, customizable workspaces, publishing options for applications, storage and database solutions, billing management, team collaboration features, and multiple access platforms including web, mobile, desktop, and chat integrations. Implement the entire system with a backend server in a single Python file using Flask for handling all API endpoints, database interactions, authentication, AI integrations, and business logic. Use SQLite for the database to store user data, projects, billing information, and other persistent data. For AI features, integrate with AWS Bedrock for Claude Opus 4.5 via boto3 for code generation and explanations, and with xAI API for Grok 4.1 fast reasoning via requests for autonomous building and fast reasoning tasks; make API calls to these services for all AI operations, using environment variables for AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID, AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY, AWS_REGION (set to ‘us-east-1’), and XAI_API_KEY. The frontend must be a single-page application contained entirely within one index.html file, using vanilla JavaScript for all interactivity, routing for subpages via hash-based navigation, and CSS for styling; include all subpages (such as home, workspace, billing, teams, docs) as hidden sections that are shown/hidden based on navigation without page reloads. Ensure the system is secure, scalable, and handles real-time collaboration using WebSockets. Follow these steps exactly to build the system from scratch:
	1	Set up the backend structure: Create a single Python file named app.py. Import necessary modules including flask, flask_socketio for WebSockets, sqlite3 for database, boto3 and json for AWS Bedrock Claude integrations, requests for xAI Grok API calls, threading for background tasks, and bcrypt for password hashing. Initialize a Flask app and SocketIO. Create an SQLite database named platform.db with tables for users (id, username, email, password_hash, plan_type, credits), projects (id, owner_id, name, files_json, visibility, snapshots_json), teams (id, name, admin_id, members_json), billing (id, user_id, usage_json, invoices_json), databases (id, project_id, schema_json, data_json), storage (id, project_id, files_json), and ai_conversations (id, project_id, history_json). Use JSON fields to store complex data structures.
	2	Implement authentication: Add endpoints for user registration (/register POST: take username, email, password; hash password with bcrypt; insert into users; return JWT token), login (/login POST: verify credentials; return JWT), and token validation middleware for protected routes. Support SSO simulation for enterprise users via a /sso endpoint that accepts SAML-like data.
	3	Build project management: Create endpoints for creating projects (/projects POST: take name, type; insert into projects with files_json as empty dict; return project_id), listing projects (/projects GET: filter by owner_id), updating projects (/projects/{id} PUT: update name, files_json), deleting projects (/projects/{id} DELETE), and importing projects (/import POST: accept source like GitHub URL or ZIP; parse and insert as new project). Support visibility settings (public/private) and collaboration via join links (/join/{link} GET: add user to project collaborators).
	4	Integrate AI tools: Implement an /ai/agent endpoint (POST: take prompt, project_id; use xAI Grok 4.1 API for fast reasoning to plan and reason about the task, then use AWS Bedrock Claude Opus 4.5 to generate code based on the reasoning output; update project files_json; create checkpoints in snapshots_json; bill credits based on effort). For the Grok call, use requests.post to  with headers {‘Authorization’: ’Bearer ’ + os.getenv(‘XAI_API_KEY’), ‘Content-Type’: ‘application/json’}, and body {‘model’: ‘grok-4.1-fast’, ‘messages’: [{‘role’: ‘system’, ‘content’: ‘You are a fast reasoning AI for planning code tasks.’}, {‘role’: ‘user’, ‘content’: prompt}]}; parse response[‘choices’][0][‘message’][‘content’]. For Claude, use boto3.client(‘bedrock-runtime’, region_name=os.getenv(‘AWS_REGION’, ‘us-east-1’)) with credentials from environment, invoke_model with modelId=‘anthropic.claude-opus-4.5-v1:0’, body=json.dumps({‘anthropic_version’: ‘bedrock-2023-05-31’, ‘max_tokens’: 4096, ‘temperature’: 0.7, ‘messages’: [{‘role’: ‘user’, ‘content’: [{‘type’: ‘text’, ‘text’: reasoned_prompt}]}]}); parse the response body. Add /ai/assistant (POST: take mode basic/advanced, prompt; for basic, use Claude for explanations; for advanced, use Grok for reasoning and Claude to modify files_json and create edit requests). Support autonomy levels, app testing (simulate browser interactions via API), and build modes (fast/full). Include integrations for payments (/ai/stripe), image generation (/ai/image using Claude for description then separate image API if needed), and web search (/ai/search using Grok for querying).
	5	Develop workspace features: Add WebSocket rooms for real-time collaboration on projects (emit ‘edit’ for file changes, ‘run’ for execution). Endpoints for workspace tools like /console (GET/POST: run code and stream output), /shell (WebSocket: command-line interaction), /preview (GET: render web app preview), /secrets (POST/GET: manage encrypted keys), /version_control (GET/POST: handle file history and git-like operations), /workflows (POST: define run commands).
	6	Implement publishing and deployments: Endpoints for publishing (/publish/{project_id} POST: take type autoscale/static/reserved/scheduled; create snapshot; deploy to simulated cloud; return URL). Support custom domains (/domains POST), analytics (/analytics/{deploy_id} GET), and monitoring (/status/{deploy_id} GET). Bill based on usage (compute, storage).
	7	Add storage and databases: Endpoints for databases (/db/{project_id} POST/GET/PUT: manage schema and data in databases table), app storage (/storage/{project_id} POST/GET: upload/download files to storage table). Support SDK-like access via API.
	8	Handle billing and plans: Endpoints for plans (/plans GET: list starter/core/teams/enterprise), upgrade (/upgrade POST: update user plan_type, add credits), usage (/usage GET: calculate from billing table), invoices (/invoices GET), alerts (/alerts POST: set thresholds). Implement credit system with monthly resets.
	9	Support teams and enterprise: Endpoints for teams (/teams POST: create team), members (/teams/{id}/members POST/GET), projects (/teams/{id}/projects), and enterprise features like /security (GET: compliance info), /iam (POST: manage roles, SCIM).
	10	Integrate platforms: Add simulated endpoints for mobile/desktop apps (/api/mobile, /api/desktop: sync projects), chat integration (/api/chat: build apps via prompts using both AIs), and CLUI (/clui POST: execute commands like account management).
	11	Set up frontend: Create index.html with sections for home (project creation, imports), workspace (file tree, editor, tools dock, run button), ai-chat (prompt input, history), publishing (options, analytics), billing (usage dashboard, invoices), teams (management, profiles), docs (navigation, content from provided descriptions). Use JavaScript for routing (e.g., window.onhashchange to show/hide sections), real-time updates via SocketIO client, API calls with fetch, and forms for inputs. Include CSS for responsive design, themes, and embeds.
	12	Ensure security and extras: Implement rate limiting, input validation, encryption for secrets. Add features like themes (/themes POST), keyboard shortcuts (event listeners), multiplayer cursors (WebSocket), and FAQ/cheat sheet sections.
	13	Test and finalize: Ensure all features work end-to-end, handle errors gracefully, and optimize for production. Output the complete code: first the full app.py contents, then the complete index.html contents including all embedded JS and CSS.




